(>> 4.3 Jacobians and Functional Transformations) Tj
T*
/F1 9 Tf
() Tj
T*
(* Definition of Jacobian: If u = u\(x, y\) and v = v\(x, y\) are two differentiable) Tj
T*
(functions, the Jacobian of \(u, v\) with respect to \(x, y\), denoted J = d\(u, v\) / d\(x,) Tj
T*
(y\), is the determinant:) Tj
T*
(J = det\([ du/dx du/dy ; dv/dx dv/dy ]\) = \(du/dx\)*\(dv/dy\) - \(du/dy\)*\(dv/dx\).) Tj
T*
(* Fundamental Properties of Jacobians:) Tj
T*
(1. Reciprocal Property: J * J' = 1, where J = d\(u, v\)/d\(x, y\) and J' = d\(x, y\)/d\(u, v\).) Tj
T*
(2. Chain Rule Property: If u, v are functions of r, s, and r, s are functions of x, y,) Tj
T*
(then d\(u, v\)/d\(x, y\) = [ d\(u, v\)/d\(r, s\) ] * [ d\(r, s\)/d\(x, y\) ].) Tj
T*
(3. Functional Dependence Test: Two functions u\(x, y\) and v\(x, y\) are functionally) Tj
T*
(dependent \(i.e., there exists a relation F\(u, v\) = 0\) if and only if their Jacobian) Tj
T*
(identically vanishes: d\(u, v\) / d\(x, y\) = 0.) Tj
T*
